    "sections": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Show People** (1928) stands as one of silent cinema's greatest meta-commentaries on Hollywood itself. Director King Vidor crafted a brilliant satire following Marion Davies as Peggy Pepper, a Georgia girl seeking stardom in Hollywood. The film mercilessly lampoons the industry's pretensions while celebrating its magic, predating similar works like <a href=\"/movie/599\" class=\"movie-title\">Sunset Boulevard</a> (1950) and **Singin' in the Rain** (1952) by decades. Davies, often unfairly remembered only as William Randolph Hearst's mistress, delivers a masterclass in physical comedy and dramatic range, proving herself one of the era's most versatile performers."
      },
